{artist: Emerson, Lake, and Palmer }
{composer: Greg Lake }


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=89g1P_J40JA  (Capo 0)

This song is special to me as it’s the very first song I ever learned to play way back in nineteen hundred and seventy-five.  It’s easy to play, especially if you skip all the little flourishes.  But they're not hard to play at all.  

TIP:  In the intro, if you concentrate on twinkling the bottom three strings you can get pretty close to what Greg Lake intended.

TIP:  If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (Gadd) and add the 5th note as necessary.

TIP:  The "lucky man" flourish comes first:  It's just a very quick one-stroke transition move from (G) to (Gadd) between "luck" and "y".  Play the 1st string (G) with your pinky and just hammer and hold the ring finger on the 2nd string.  Or skip the (G) completely and play (Gadd) prior to "luck".  Also note the very quick transition from the (Em) prior to "he" and the (D) prior to "was".  You can skip this (Em) completely if you want.
 
TIP:  The (D) (D4) (Dsus2) - (D) part immediately follows:  The (D) comes first with "was" followed by the (D4) - which some call the (Dsus4) but we'll call it a (D4) to keep things simple - for which you just add your pinky to the 1st string/3rd fret.  Lift off to get back to (G) and then lift the middle finger off the 1st string/2nd fret for one stroke (that's what the dash "-" between the (Dsus2) and (D) means) and replace it to return to (D).  Couldn’t be easier!  

TIP:  At the end the lads play the "aww" outro 5 times followed by more Moog and Carl's drums.  I've included one way to end the song, the way I did when I played the O2 (yeah right). 

TIP:  If you have a friend to play the kazoo for the Moog part at the end that would be like, way cool.


